WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.700 --> 00:00:06.807
Psychosocial risks
are characteristics of work,

2
00:00:06.890 --> 00:00:08.637
such as the way it is organised,

3
00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:12.377
the content of tasks
or the quality of relationships,

4
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:17.184
that can harm people's physical,
emotional and social health.

5
00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:19.517
Although they are not directly visible,

6
00:00:19.600 --> 00:00:22.297
like broken equipment or an unsafe space,

7
00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:25.737
their effects are real and often profound.

8
00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:28.457
Prolonged exposure to these risks

9
00:00:28.540 --> 00:00:31.727
is associated with higher
levels of stress, burnout,

10
00:00:31.810 --> 00:00:34.707
anxiety, depression
and also physical symptoms,

11
00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:38.183
such as muscle pain or sleep problems.

12
00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:42.187
Among the most common
examples are work overload,

13
00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:45.567
constant pressure for results,
lack of recognition,

14
00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:50.980
insecurity about the future,
or conflicts with colleagues and managers.

15
00:00:51.500 --> 00:00:56.047
When these situations persist
over time, they can contribute

16
00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:59.024
to toxic and harmful working environments.

17
00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:04.207
It should be stressed that these risks
are not limited to specific sectors;

18
00:01:04.290 --> 00:01:07.937
they can exist in any area,
role or organisation.

19
00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:12.677
For this reason, they must be recognised,
assessed and managed systematically,

20
00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:15.638
just like any other occupational risk.

21
00:01:16.020 --> 00:01:18.417
Creating psychologically
safe working environments

22
00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:20.457
is not just an ethical obligation,

23
00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:23.607
it is also an effective strategy
for promoting well-being

24
00:01:23.690 --> 00:01:26.708
and improving organisational performance.

25
00:01:27.020 --> 00:01:30.297
So let's talk about the types
of psychosocial risk at work.

26
00:01:30.380 --> 00:01:32.400
First, job demands.

27
00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:35.157
Job demands refer to the volume

28
00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.702
and intensity of the tasks we are given.

29
00:01:38.980 --> 00:01:41.847
This dimension includes
quantitative demands,

30
00:01:41.930 --> 00:01:43.717
which concern the amount of work,

31
00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.674
tight deadlines and excessive workload.

32
00:01:47.580 --> 00:01:49.327
A high and constant pace of work

33
00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:52.606
can create a sense of permanent urgency.

34
00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:57.457
Cognitive demands involve staying
focused, making quick decisions,

35
00:01:57.540 --> 00:02:01.447
handling complex information,
and emotional demands involve,

36
00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:04.377
for example,
dealing with other people's suffering,

37
00:02:04.460 --> 00:02:07.737
hiding emotions or managing
frequent conflicts.

38
00:02:07.820 --> 00:02:12.207
When these demands are constant
and not matched by resources or support,

39
00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:15.537
they can lead to physical
and emotional exhaustion.

40
00:02:15.620 --> 00:02:19.810
The second type of risk is work
organisation and content.

41
00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:25.077
Here we look at how work is structured
and at the content of tasks.

42
00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:28.007
This dimension includes influence at work,

43
00:02:28.090 --> 00:02:30.577
which concerns the degree of autonomy:

44
00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:33.837
can we make decisions
or only carry out orders?

45
00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:36.247
The possibility of development,

46
00:02:36.330 --> 00:02:39.627
which refers to opportunities for learning

47
00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:41.517
and growth. The meaning of work,

48
00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:45.537
which relates to the perception
that what we do has value.

49
00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:49.127
Commitment to the workplace,
reflected in involvement,

50
00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:54.037
identification with the organisation's
goals and a sense of belonging.

51
00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:57.137
And environments that do not allow growth

52
00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:59.387
or recognise the value of the work done

53
00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:02.217
tend to create frustration
and demotivation.

54
00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:06.037
The third type of risk is social
relations and leadership.

55
00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.197
This is one of the most important
areas for mental health at work.

56
00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:12.937
This dimension includes predictability,

57
00:03:13.020 --> 00:03:17.275
which relates to clarity
of roles and expectations.

58
00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:19.567
Reward can be financial,

59
00:03:19.650 --> 00:03:23.057
but it also includes
recognition and appreciation.

60
00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:25.747
Transparency is therefore
essential for trust:

61
00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:28.537
knowing what is expected of us,
how we are assessed,

62
00:03:28.620 --> 00:03:30.637
what decisions are being made.

63
00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:33.747
Role conflict arises when there
are contradictory demands

64
00:03:33.830 --> 00:03:36.837
or a lack of clearly
defined responsibilities.

65
00:03:36.920 --> 00:03:39.707
The quality of leadership is decisive.

66
00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:45.017
Authoritarian, absent or inconsistent
leadership creates tension and insecurity.

67
00:03:45.100 --> 00:03:49.357
Social support from colleagues
and managers acts as a protective factor.

68
00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:52.807
When there is empathy,
availability and collaboration,

69
00:03:52.890 --> 00:03:55.017
the impact of stress tends to be smaller.

70
00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.957
Poor or toxic relationships
at work are one of the main

71
00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:02.497
causes of emotional distress
and of people leaving their jobs.

72
00:04:02.580 --> 00:04:06.513
The fourth type of risk
is the work-individual interface.

73
00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.037
This type of risk relates to the way

74
00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:11.430
work fits in with other areas of life.

75
00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:14.807
This dimension includes job insecurity,

76
00:04:14.890 --> 00:04:18.267
such as fear of losing one's
job or unstable contracts,

77
00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:22.217
which causes prolonged anxiety.
Job satisfaction,

78
00:04:22.300 --> 00:04:26.937
which is an important indicator
of well-being: if we are dissatisfied,

79
00:04:27.020 --> 00:04:31.137
symptoms of burnout or demotivation
are more likely to appear.

80
00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:33.097
Work-family conflict arises

81
00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:37.027
when professional demands
interfere with personal life,

82
00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.717
leaving too little time
to be with the children,

83
00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:42.189
rest or look after one's health.

84
00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:45.337
These are some of the most
concrete examples.

85
00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:48.927
Environments that do not respect
this balance undermine

86
00:04:49.010 --> 00:04:52.077
workers' health and their productivity
in the medium term.

87
00:04:52.160 --> 00:04:56.257
The fifth type of psychosocial
risk is values in the workplace.

88
00:04:56.340 --> 00:04:59.637
Organisational culture strongly influences

89
00:04:59.720 --> 00:05:02.117
how people feel in their day-to-day lives.

90
00:05:02.200 --> 00:05:05.557
This dimension includes
vertical trust, that is,

91
00:05:05.640 --> 00:05:08.597
between those who lead
and those who are led,

92
00:05:08.680 --> 00:05:11.997
and this trust is essential
for good communication

93
00:05:12.080 --> 00:05:15.178
and for resolving conflicts and problems.

94
00:05:15.320 --> 00:05:17.967
Horizontal trust, that is,
between colleagues,

95
00:05:18.050 --> 00:05:21.257
makes collaboration
and team spirit easier.

96
00:05:21.340 --> 00:05:24.027
Justice and respect
involve fair treatment,

97
00:05:24.110 --> 00:05:28.177
recognition of differences
and active listening.

98
00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:33.077
Social community at work refers
to the quality of informal relationships,

99
00:05:33.160 --> 00:05:37.557
the sense of belonging,
the idea that we are part of something.

100
00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:40.467
When values are out of step with practice

101
00:05:40.550 --> 00:05:42.497
or when there is institutional hypocrisy,

102
00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:47.794
the effects are corrosive to employees'
motivation and mental health.

103
00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:50.857
The sixth type of risk
is offensive behaviour.

104
00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:55.377
This is one of the most serious risks
and one of the hardest to report.

105
00:05:55.460 --> 00:06:00.117
This dimension includes bullying,
with repeated humiliation,

106
00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:03.628
belittling, sabotage or social isolation.

107
00:06:04.500 --> 00:06:07.467
Sexual harassment, with unwanted comments,

108
00:06:07.550 --> 00:06:11.057
gestures or behaviour of a sexual nature.

109
00:06:11.140 --> 00:06:16.637
Discrimination based on gender,
ethnicity, sexual orientation, age,

110
00:06:16.720 --> 00:06:19.593
disability, among others.

111
00:06:20.020 --> 00:06:24.505
Verbal or physical violence,
even if subtle or disguised.

112
00:06:24.860 --> 00:06:28.937
These behaviours we have talked
about violate basic human rights

113
00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:32.237
and have a devastating impact
on mental health and self-esteem.

114
00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:34.807
Psychosocial risks are not inevitable,

115
00:06:34.890 --> 00:06:38.417
but they arise when work
is poorly planned or managed,

116
00:06:38.500 --> 00:06:42.606
or when the social context
does not protect the worker.

117
00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:47.487
Identifying them is the first step
to preventing psychological distress

118
00:06:47.570 --> 00:06:50.060
and promoting healthier workplaces.
